        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Load one card, sweep it\n\nTwo sweeps off the same image, so you can see a parameter's shape instead of guessing at it.\n\n### The distinction that matters\n\n- **`seed` = -1, `sweep_param` = none, `variations` = 8** \u2014 eight different images. This is *variation*: you are sampling the space.\n- **`seed` fixed, `sweep_param` = guidance** \u2014 one image per value with everything else nailed down. This is *a sweep*: you are measuring one axis.\n\nMixing them tells you nothing, because you cannot tell which change caused what. The top lane sweeps `guidance` on a fixed seed. The bottom lane sweeps `steps` on the *same* fixed seed, so the two ladders are directly comparable.\n\n`variations` multiplies on top of a sweep: 5 values \u00d7 2 variations = 10 calls.\n\n### sweep_spec\n- explicit list \u2014 `3,5,7,9`\n- inclusive range \u2014 `1.5..8`\n- range with step \u2014 `2..8:1.5`\n\n`steps` and `guidance` exist only on **flux-2-flex**. `seed`, `width`, `height` and `safety_tolerance` sweep on any model.\n\n### Reading the results\nEach contact sheet prints the swept value under its tile. `output_dir` also writes every frame to disk with the value and seed in the filename, plus a `manifest.txt` \u2014 so a sheet you like six weeks from now is still traceable to its settings.\n\n### Cost and speed\n`parallel` keeps that many calls in flight. The calls are network-bound, so 4 lanes is roughly 4\u00d7 faster in wall-clock and costs exactly the same. The node refuses any run over 100 calls.\n\nThe **Cost / Call Report** at the right totals what the session actually spent, and says how many results came from cache rather than the API \u2014 a re-run with the same fixed seed is free."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 variations and sweeps",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
